    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d139">

                <pb facs="0326" n="236" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg236"/>

                <head>No. 135.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Avery to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-12-17"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 17, 1874</date>.
                        (Received February 24, 1875.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 13.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: As illustrating the happy effects of a
                    judicious but firm insistance upon strict compliance with treaty obligations on
                    the part of local Chinese officials, I have the honor to inclose copy of a
                    letter from our consul at Tien-tsin, covering a voluntary testimonial from the
                    Rev. John Maclntyre, a British missionary, as to the good results of the
                    settlement of the Chimi affair by Mr. Sheppard. Your approval of the latter’s
                    course has already been signified to him.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>BENJ. P. AVERY.</signed></closer>




                <xi:include href="d139-a1.xml" xpointer="d139-a1"/>
